---
import CaseStudy from '../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
---

<CaseStudy title="Experience">
  <div class="meta-row">
    <p class="tag">2021 – Present</p>
    <div>
      <h2 class="type-h3">Head of Design at <a href="https://www.stockopedia.com/">Stockopedia</a></h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Lead the design team and manage the backlog whilst helping to shape the product strategy and long-term vision.</li>
        <li>Successfully developed and drove adoption of a design and discovery process from the ground up.</li>
        <li>Regularly consulted by the business to help map and resolve complex process issues.</li>
        <li>Execute all UX design work, ensuring a seamless and consistent experience across every channel and customer journey.</li>
        <li>Established a structured recruitment process for the business, which has increased objectivity, reduced the duration of the process and improved the quality of hires.</li>
        <li>My UX work has contributed to achieving an “Excellent” Net Promoter Score, with “Ease of use” being the second most frequently stated promoter comment.</li>
        <li>Since joining Stockopedia, the platform has been recognised with industry awards for the Best Research Service and Best Investment Tool.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="meta-row">
    <p class="tag">2018 – 2021</p>
    <div>
      <h2 class="type-h3">UX and UI Designer, <a href="https://www.stockopedia.com/">Stockopedia</a></h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Integrated user research into product development process through user tests, customer feedback analysis, and examination of usage data.</li>
        <li>Created prototypes for ideation, alignment, and testing, as well as high-fidelity UI specifications for development.</li>
        <li>Established a Design System to ensure consistency and improve the efficiency of the design-developement process.</li>
        <li>Contributed to a successful application redesign, resulting in the migration of over 15,000 subscribers.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="meta-row">
    <p class="tag">2015 – 2018</p>
    <div>
      <h2 class="type-h3">UI and Visual Designer, <a href="https://manufacture2030.com/">M2030</a></h2>
      <p>I designed the Manufacture 2030 platform from an initial concept (driving corporate sustainability), developing brand guidelines and a component library. I also worked on the 2degrees platform and public-facing website for the sustainable business community. Key milestones included working with major suppliers such as Asda-Walmart, GSK, and Unilever, being named in Sustainia’s Top 100, and joining the UN Global Opportunity Explorer.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="meta-row">
    <p class="tag">2014 – 2015</p>
    <div>
      <h2 class="type-h3">UX Designer: Secondary, <a href="https://www.pearson.com/en-gb.html">Pearson Plc</a></h2>
      <p>I was the sole Designer for ExamWizard, an online assessment tool specialising in customisable exam papers aligned with educational curricula. I oversaw the entire design process, from wireframes and user testing, to high-fidelity interface designs.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="meta-row">
    <p class="tag">2013 – 2014</p>
    <div>
      <h2 class="type-h3">UI Designer: Academic &amp; General, <a href="https://www.pearson.com/en-gb.html">Pearson Plc</a></h2>
      <p>I led the design of ActiveTeach, a platform that integrates digital resources for students with front-of-class teaching materials to improve classroom engagement. My primary role was user interface design, with additional responsibilities in UX design and front-end development. I also designed the Pearson Portal, a single sign-on solution for users accessing multiple Pearson products and services.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="meta-row">
    <p class="tag">2012 – 2013</p>
    <div>
      <h2 class="type-h3">Managing Designer: Primary, <a href="https://www.pearson.com/en-gb.html">Pearson Plc</a></h2>
      <p>I worked on the award-winning children’s reading programs Bug Club and Wordsmith, which encompass both print publishing and interactive eBooks. My role involved commissioning illustrators, overseeing freelance designers, and providing art direction across various primary education projects. In-house design tasks included storyboarding, typesetting, illustration, vector graphic creation, and creative typography.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="meta-row">
    <p class="tag">2012</p>
    <div>
      <h2 class="type-h3">Graphic Designer (Internship), <a href="https://www.libertylondon.com/">Liberty Ltd</a></h2>
      <p>I worked in the Graphics studio of this renowned London department store, producing creative design solutions whilst adhering to the Liberty brand guidelines. My work included point of sale displays, events and marketing promotions, product packaging and typographic clothing designs.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  /* .tag and .meta-row are shared utilities in global.css. This page just
     stacks a run of them — one per role — with the same rhythm About Me
     uses for its section breaks. */
  /* Spacing runs one direction only. padding-block on every row meant adjacent
     rows each contributed their own — and padding doesn't collapse the way
     margins do — so the gap between roles was double the value set here. */
  .meta-row + .meta-row {
    margin-top: var(--gap-block);
  }

  .meta-row:first-of-type {
    margin-top: var(--gap-block);
  }

  /* Capped to the same measure as the body copy below it. Unconstrained,
     these run to the full ~716px content column — invisible on Step, whose
     titles are always one or two words, but Experience's headings are full
     "role, company" strings, so at type-h3 size they were wrapping wider
     than the paragraphs beneath them and looking uncontrolled. */
  /* Heading-to-body is a tight, within-block relationship rather than a
     gap between independent sections, so it takes a fixed inset (--space-16)
     rather than the fluid --gap-inner — the same reasoning, and the same
     value, as the hero's byline-to-intro gap. */
  .meta-row h2 {
    max-width: var(--content-width);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-16);
  }

  ul {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: 0.75em;
  }

  li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
